steps.body:
Overload `operator()` and objects of your struct can be called like functions. Such a struct is a **function object** (a functor):

```cpp
struct ByLength {
    bool operator()(const std::string& a, const std::string& b) const {
        if (a.size() != b.size()) {
            return a.size() < b.size();
        }
        return a < b;
    }
};
```

`ByLength{}` creates one, and you can pass it wherever a lambda goes: `std::sort(v.begin(), v.end(), ByLength{})`. The same comparator rules apply: return true when `a` goes before `b`, use `<` never `<=`, and break ties.

The big advantage over a lambda is that a function object has a named **type**. A set or map takes its comparator as a template argument, so you can write:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

struct ByLength {
    bool operator()(const std::string& a, const std::string& b) const {
        if (a.size() != b.size()) {
            return a.size() < b.size();
        }
        return a < b;
    }
};

int main() {
    {{code}}
    for (const std::string& w : {"pear", "fig", "apple", "kiwi", "fig"}) {
        words.insert(w);
        ++counts[w];
    }
    for (const auto& w : words) {
        std::cout << w << ' ';
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
    for (const auto& [w, n] : counts) {
        std::cout << w << ' ' << n << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
std::set<std::string, ByLength> words;
std::map<std::string, int, ByLength> counts;
```

and every insert and lookup uses your ordering. Equality follows the comparator too: with a comparator that only looked at length, `"fig"` and `"cat"` would count as the same key. That is why `ByLength` breaks ties alphabetically.

`std::greater<int>` from `<functional>` is a standard function object that returns `a > b`, so `std::set<int, std::greater<int>>` iterates from largest to smallest.

example.code:
#include <algorithm>
#include <functional>
#include <iostream>
#include <set>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

struct ByLength {
    bool operator()(const std::string& a, const std::string& b) const {
        if (a.size() != b.size()) {
            return a.size() < b.size();
        }
        return a < b;
    }
};

template <typename C>
void printLine(const C& items) {
    bool first = true;
    for (const auto& item : items) {
        if (!first) {
            std::cout << ' ';
        }
        std::cout << item;
        first = false;
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
}

int main() {
    std::vector<std::string> words = {"pear", "fig", "apple", "kiwi", "fig"};
    std::sort(words.begin(), words.end(), ByLength{});
    printLine(words);

    const std::set<std::string, ByLength> distinct(words.begin(), words.end());
    printLine(distinct);

    std::set<int, std::greater<int>> lengths;
    for (const auto& w : words) {
        lengths.insert(static_cast<int>(w.size()));
    }
    printLine(lengths);
    return 0;
}

